---
title: Steak with pan sauce
description: Lesson 1.3. Searing, and a sauce from what the sear leaves in the pan.
servings: 2
time: 30 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, heat-in-a-pan]
lesson: "1.3"
technique: searing and deglazing
status: draft
---

> Technique: searing, then deglazing. This pan starts hot, the opposite
> of lesson 1.1. The steak browns fast and leaves a brown layer stuck to
> the pan. That layer is called fond, and the sauce is made from it.
-- source: "Pan Sauces 101", America's Test Kitchen / Cook's Illustrated, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/cooksillustrated/how_tos/5551-pan-sauces-101

Pat @sirloin steaks{2}(about 250 g each, 2.5 cm thick) dry with kitchen paper and season both sides with @salt{1%tsp}.

Heat @oil{1%tbsp}(sunflower or rapeseed) in a #frying pan{}(stainless steel or cast iron, not non-stick) over high heat until the oil just starts to smoke.

> Why this pan: a non-stick pan does not build up fond the way a plain
> steel or iron pan does, so it gives a weaker sauce.

Lay the steaks in the pan and cook for ~{3%minutes} a side, turning once, until both sides are deep brown and the centre reads 63 °C on a #thermometer{}.

Move the steaks to a plate and leave them to rest for ~{5%minutes} while you make the sauce.

> Why: a steak keeps cooking after it leaves the pan and climbs a few
> more degrees, so the temperature you read in the pan is not where it
> ends up. The old reason for resting, that juices soak back in, is weak:
> the measured difference in lost juice is small and disputed.
-- source: Meathead Goldwyn with Greg Blonder, "The Science of Resting Meat", AmazingRibs.com, https://amazingribs.com/more-technique-and-science/more-cooking-science/science-of-resting-meat/

> Safety: the USDA's safe minimum for steak is 63 °C, read before it
> leaves the heat, then a rest of at least 3 minutes. This recipe
> follows it. Medium-rare, about 55 °C, is below that figure; many
> cooks choose it anyway.
-- source: USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service, "Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart", last updated 2025-04-14: steaks 145 °F (62.8 °C), measured before removing from the heat, and rest at least 3 minutes, https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart

Pour off all but a thin film of fat from the pan, keeping the brown layer. Set the pan over medium heat, add @shallot{1}(finely chopped) and stir for ~{1%minute}, until soft.

Pour in @red wine{75%ml} and @beef stock{150%ml}. Scrape the bottom of the pan with a #wooden spoon{} until the brown layer has dissolved into the liquid and the pan bottom is clean. Simmer for ~{5%minutes}, until the liquid has reduced by about half and coats the back of the spoon.

Take the pan off the heat. Stir in @cold butter{30%g}(cut into 3 pieces) one piece at a time, then pour in any juice from the steak plate. Taste and add salt if it needs it.

Slice the steaks across the grain and spoon the sauce over.

> Next time, without the recipe: sear anything in a plain steel pan,
> then look at what is stuck to it. Soften something small and sharp
> (shallot, garlic), add a liquid, scrape, boil it down, and finish off
> the heat with cold butter.
